 be Chum din they have no interest  as a rule in coming close to the boat  the researchers up there have used all  kinds of you know attractants and things  and nothing works to get them close to  the boat so my first season up there the  best I could do was with a pole cam  this is a picture I made while our boat  was underway of one of these giant great  white sharks in that very murky water  there's lots of currents you couldn't  use a cage there you wouldn't see  anything and the currents would blow it  over  pretty impressive tail zone right sure  but I eventually figured that what I  needed to do was build a seal decoy so  so I went back to my friends at Woods  Hole and we built this big grey seal we  try to make it as authentic as possible  like an adult of gray CLI skull had the  head sculpted by a model maker and we  put cameras inside with fiber optic  cables and you know I could watch in  real time and live view back on the boat  we had a little wetsuit carved like a  seal body here and we would deploy it  day and night day and night day and  night and nothing sharks had absolutely  no interest they were laughing as they  gave me the finger as they swam by and  that was about it  so over the winter I went home dejected  and you know wasn't sure I was going to  have a story I didn't know if I'd get  any pictures but over the winter I  talked to a friend of mine who's a  veterinarian on Cape Cod and also a  shark researcher and he had also been  working on his own seal decoy design and  he had perfected it he got it to a point  where it was a much simpler design a  much smaller seal design only about  maybe 3 or 4 feet in length and he said  he was having a little bit of success  with this so I took his design and I  adapted my cameras to what I needed to  do was put a wireless underwater camera  in there that I could trigger from  hundreds of yards away I didn't want any  cables in the water and I also had to  have real-time video in there as well  that I 